| `-FullComment 0x326f1f10 <line:31:3, line:67:93>
|   |-ParagraphComment 0x326f1960 <line:31:3, line:34:13>
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f18c0 <line:31:3, col:100> Text=" In C++ an unsigned to signed cast where the source value cannot be represented in the destination"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f18e0 <line:32:3, col:100> Text=" type results in an implementation defined destination value. Unlike C, C++ does not allow a trap."
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1900 <line:33:3, col:95> Text=" This makes "(S)(D)s == s" a possibly useful test. However, there are two cases where this is"
|   | `-TextComment 0x326f1920 <line:34:3, col:13> Text=" incorrect:"
|   |-ParagraphComment 0x326f19d0 <line:36:3, line:37:87>
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1980 <line:36:3, col:96> Text=" when testing if a value of a smaller signed type can be represented in a larger unsigned type"
|   | `-TextComment 0x326f19a0 <line:37:3, col:87> Text=" (int8_t)(uint16_t)-1 == -1 => (int8_t)0xFFFF == -1 => [implementation defined] == -1"
|   |-ParagraphComment 0x326f1a40 <line:39:3, line:40:92>
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f19f0 <line:39:3, col:96> Text=" when testing if a value of a larger unsigned type can be represented in a smaller signed type"
|   | `-TextComment 0x326f1a10 <line:40:3, col:92> Text=" (uint16_t)(int8_t)0xFFFF == 0xFFFF => (uint16_t)-1 == 0xFFFF => 0xFFFF == 0xFFFF => true."
|   |-ParagraphComment 0x326f1b50 <line:42:3, line:47:38>
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1a60 <line:42:3, col:22> Text=" Consider the cases:"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1a80 <line:43:3, col:30> Text="   u = unsigned, less digits"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1aa0 <line:44:3, col:30> Text="   U = unsigned, more digits"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1ac0 <line:45:3, col:28> Text="   s = signed, less digits"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1ae0 <line:46:3, col:28> Text="   S = signed, more digits"
|   | `-TextComment 0x326f1b00 <line:47:3, col:38> Text="   v is the value we're considering."
|   |-ParagraphComment 0x326f1dd0 <line:49:3, line:63:38>
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1b70 <line:49:3, col:39> Text=" u -> U: (u)(U)v == v, trivially true"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1b90 <line:50:3, col:60> Text=" U -> u: (U)(u)v == v, both casts well defined, test works"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1bb0 <line:51:3, col:39> Text=" s -> S: (s)(S)v == v, trivially true"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1bd0 <line:52:3, col:89> Text=" S -> s: (S)(s)v == v, first cast implementation value, second cast defined, test works"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1bf0 <line:53:3, col:95> Text=" s -> U: (s)(U)v == v, *this is bad*, the second cast results in implementation defined value"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1c10 <line:54:3, col:92> Text=" S -> u: (S)(u)v == v, the second cast is required to prevent promotion of rhs to unsigned"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1c30 <line:55:3, col:39> Text=" u -> S: (u)(S)v == v, trivially true"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1c50 <line:56:3, col:39> Text=" U -> s: (U)(s)v == v, *this is bad*,"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1c70 <line:57:3, col:82> Text="                             first cast results in implementation defined value,"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1c90 <line:58:3, col:92> Text="                             second cast is defined. However, this creates false positives"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1cb0 <line:59:3, col:50> Text="                             uint16_t x = 0xFFFF"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1cd0 <line:60:3, col:58> Text="                                (uint16_t)(int8_t)x == x"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1cf0 <line:61:3, col:58> Text="                             => (uint16_t)-1        == x"
|   | |-TextComment 0x326f1d10 <line:62:3, col:58> Text="                             => 0xFFFF              == x"
|   | `-TextComment 0x326f1d30 <line:63:3, col:38> Text="                             => true"
|   `-ParagraphComment 0x326f1ec0 <line:65:3, line:67:93>
|     |-TextComment 0x326f1df0 <line:65:3, col:100> Text=" So for the eight cases three are trivially true, three more are valid casts, and two are special."
|     |-TextComment 0x326f1e10 <line:66:3, col:87> Text=" The two 'full' checks which otherwise require two comparisons are valid cast checks."
|     |-TextComment 0x326f1e30 <line:67:3, col:58> Text=" The two remaining checks s -> U [v >= 0] and U -> s [v "
