| | | |-ParagraphComment 0x326f51e0 <line:34:3, line:36:23>
| | | | |-TextComment 0x326f5160 <line:34:3, col:96> Text=" SkSpan holds a reference to contiguous data of type T along with a count. SkSpan does not own"
| | | | |-TextComment 0x326f5180 <line:35:3, col:95> Text=" the data itself but is merely a reference, therefore you must take care with the lifetime of"
| | | | `-TextComment 0x326f51a0 <line:36:3, col:23> Text=" the underlying data."
| | | |-ParagraphComment 0x326f5280 <line:38:3, line:40:15>
| | | | |-TextComment 0x326f5200 <line:38:3, col:91> Text=" SkSpan is a count and a pointer into existing array or data type that stores its data in"
| | | | |-TextComment 0x326f5220 <line:39:3, col:96> Text=" contiguous memory like std::vector. Any container that works with std::size() and std::data()"
| | | | `-TextComment 0x326f5240 <line:40:3, col:15> Text=" can be used."
| | | |-ParagraphComment 0x326f52f0 <line:42:3, line:43:73>
| | | | |-TextComment 0x326f52a0 <line:42:3, col:100> Text=" SkSpan makes a convenient parameter for a routine to accept array like things. This allows you to"
| | | | `-TextComment 0x326f52c0 <line:43:3, col:73> Text=" write the routine without overloads for all different container types."
| | | |-ParagraphComment 0x326f53b0 <line:45:3, line:46:48>
| | | | |-TextComment 0x326f5310 <line:45:3, col:11> Text=" Example:"
| | | | |-TextComment 0x326f5330 <line:46:3, col:26> Text="     void routine(SkSpan"
| | | | |-TextComment 0x326f5350 <col:27, col:32> Text="<const"
